# Containerfile.schema
#
# Companion document for `Containerfile`, the build of the agent host image.
#
# ## The build arguments that matter
#
# | Argument | Value |
# |----------|-------|
# | `AGENT_BASE_REF` | The base image as **one complete reference**, in either form: `<registry>/<namespace>/<name>@sha256:<manifest list digest>` for a base that was published, or `<name>:<version>-<commit>` for one built on this machine and never pushed. A single `FROM` cannot take an optional `@`, so the reference is passed whole rather than assembled from a name and a digest. Use the digest form whenever the base came from a registry — a tag moves, and a per-platform digest breaks the other architecture's build. |
# | `HERDR_SHA256_AMD64` | The publisher's SHA-256 for the `linux-x86_64` release asset at `HERDR_VERSION` (`P-4`). |
# | `HERDR_SHA256_ARM64` | The same for `linux-aarch64` (`P-5`). |
#
# The two checksum arguments default to the pinned pair for `HERDR_VERSION` —
# the same two values `P-4` and `P-5` carry — so the build command below runs as
# written. They are not free parameters to be filled in: they move **with**
# `HERDR_VERSION`, and a version bump whose digests were not re-read stops the
# build at `sha256sum -c`, which is the intended refusal rather than a step to
# work around.
#
# The base label is worth its line: `AGENT_BASE_REF` is declared before `FROM`,
# where only `FROM` can read it, so `org.opencontainers.image.base.name` needs
# the redeclaration after it. Without that, the label is an empty string in a
# built image — a defect no local check can see here, since building the layer
# needs a builder this package's verification machine does not have.
#
# There is no `org.opencontainers.image.base.digest` label any more, and its
# absence is the honest consequence of one reference: filling it would need a
# second argument that is optional exactly when the base was never published,
# which is the second argument this change removes. When the reference is the
# digest form, `...base.name` carries the digest itself; when it is a tag there
# is no digest to state, and putting a tag in a digest label would be a lie.
#
# ## What this layer may and may not change
#
# | May | Why it is in this layer's hands |
# |-----|---------------------------------|
# | the packages it installs | `openssh-server`, `openssh-client`, `jq` and `util-linux` are what the multiplexer, the attach transport and the JSON parsing need. |
# | the files it adds | the herdr binary at a pinned version, the plugin, the configuration template, the boot program. |
# | an optional `HEALTHCHECK` | Declared as none: a host's readiness is its herdr session, which the acceptance script checks directly rather than through a container health probe that would have to re-derive it. |
#
# | Must not change | Why |
# |-----------------|-----|
# | the runtime account | Its name, ids and home are the base's contract; every agent pane runs as that account. |
# | the entrypoint | `docker run <this image>` must behave exactly like the base: one agent, one workspace, the base's refusals. The host's own process is named by the deployment (`compose.service.yaml`), not by this image. |
# | the working directory | Consumers mount agent workspaces deliberately; the image default stays the base's. |
# | the `AGENT_*` variable names | They are the seam every harness layer writes through. |
# | any `EXPOSE`, `VOLUME` or socket mount | The image opens no port and mounts nothing: which port is published, and where the Docker API is reached, are the deployment's decisions. A socket mount in the image would hand every agent host root. |
#
# ## Reproducibility
#
# The base is pinned by manifest digest, herdr by version plus the publisher's
# per-asset SHA-256 checked into the build, and the package set comes from the
# base's own distribution repositories. A rebuild is the same image unless one
# of those three inputs was changed on purpose, which is what makes the layer's
# own tag meaningful.
#
# The version and both checksums are defaults carried from the release the
# package was verified against, not values to discover at build time:
# `HERDR_VERSION` (`P-3`) and the two digests (`P-4`, `P-5`) are one pin with
# three fields.
#
# ## Validation
#
# The argument with no default — `AGENT_BASE_REF` — is the only one the build
# command passes. `HERDR_VERSION` and both checksums already default to the
# pinned release, so a build either matches that release or stops at
# `sha256sum -c`.
#
# ```
# docker build -f Containerfile \
#   --build-arg AGENT_BASE_REF=<ref> \
#   -t agent-host:verify .                                          # exits 0
# docker image inspect agent-host:verify \
#   --format '{{.Config.User}} {{.Config.WorkingDir}} {{json .Config.Entrypoint}}'
#   # the base's account, working directory and entrypoint, unchanged
# docker image inspect agent-host:verify \
#   --format '{{index .Config.Labels "org.opencontainers.image.base.name"}}'
#   # the complete base reference, not the empty string the redeclaration prevents
# docker run --rm agent-host:verify                                 # the base's refusal, exit 78
# docker image inspect agent-host:verify --format '{{json .Config.ExposedPorts}}'  # null
# ```
